- op: create  # 5540
  at: '2026-04-26T05:55:07Z'  # 5540
  by: agent  # 5540
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5540
  clock: 1  # 5540
  nonce: '5540'  # 5540
  data:  # 5540
    kind: work_item  # 5540
    title: "TRACKER_SYNC_PENDING marker can leak on SIGKILL — needs fcntl.flock + stale-marker auto-recovery + vociferous failure messaging"  # 5540
    status: todo_hard  # 5540
    priority: 1  # 5540
    parent:  # 5540
    tags:  # 5540
    - bakeoff_infrastructure  # 5540
    - developer_experience  # 5540
    - invariant_violation  # 5540
    isbefore: []  # 5540
    duplicate_of: []  # 5540
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 5540
    pr_ref:  # 5540
    description: "Real bug observed 2026-04-26 during WI-gokop tag-strip work: reflect-aggregate's apply-verdicts step calls 'tracker update --remove-tag' via subprocess.run(timeout=60). That sync exceeded 60s, parent SIGKILLed the subprocess, Python's try/finally at sync.py:1201-1206 did NOT run (SIGKILL bypasses finally), and the .git/TRACKER_SYNC_PENDING marker was left stale. Subsequent ./scripts/auto-pr runs all failed with the unhelpful message 'Error: tracker sync in progress. Wait for htrac sync to complete.' until the marker was manually deleted an hour later. Mechanically this should never require manual cleanup. Required design properties (per user 2026-04-26): (1) marker MUST clean up after itself unconditionally — even on SIGKILL, even on Python crash, even on power loss. The standard pattern is fcntl.flock on an open file descriptor: the OS releases the lock when the fd closes, regardless of how the process exits. Smart-test already uses this pattern (.ci/.smart-test.lock per WI-sinap). (2) Failure detection MUST be vociferous: when a sync attempt finds the marker present, it should log loudly with marker age, holder PID (if recorded), and what to do about it — not silently refuse with a one-line cryptic message. (3) Failure handling MUST be graceful: detect stale markers (PID gone OR file age > threshold OR fcntl lock not held by anyone) and auto-recover with a logged warning, rather than blocking all subsequent operations. Suggested implementation: replace the marker-file pattern at sync.py:855 / 869 / 1009 / 1065 / 1201-1206 with an fcntl.flock-managed lock file that records the holder PID and start-timestamp inside its content for diagnostic purposes. Update auto-pr's gate checks (scripts/auto-pr:730 / 955 / 1127) to query the lock state via flock(LOCK_SH|LOCK_NB) — non-blocking attempt to take a shared lock, success means no exclusive holder. Also handle the related sync.py:670/679 'sync_pending' check in preflight similarly. Loud failure messaging should include: marker age, holder PID, holder PID alive/dead, recommended recovery action (almost always: nothing — the next sync will just retry). This is filed as P1 because it has caused real cross-session friction multiple times this week (TRACKER_SYNC_FAILED was the related but separate marker that I had to clean up earlier today; both share the no-self-cleanup-on-SIGKILL anti-pattern)."  # 5540
    fields: {}  # 5540
- op: discuss  # f8d5
  at: '2026-04-26T05:56:40Z'  # f8d5
  by: agent  # f8d5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f8d5
  clock: 2  # f8d5
  nonce: f8d5  # f8d5
  message: "Augmenting design properties (per user 2026-04-26 follow-up): the error messaging itself is part of the structural failure surface. (4) The message MUST remind the reader how to use it: 'tracker sync gate locked by PID 12345 (alive, started 2 min ago) — your operation will be retried automatically when the sync completes' (auto-recovery), or 'tracker sync gate locked by PID 12345 (DEAD, marker 1h17m old) — auto-cleared and retrying' (stale-marker self-recovery). NOT 'Error: tracker sync in progress. Wait for htrac sync to complete.' which gives the reader nothing actionable. (5) The message should inspire you to the better angels of your nature: friendly framing, no shouting (no all-caps ERROR), no jargon ('htrac' is opaque). Aim for the 'oh, the system noticed and handled it' tone, not the 'something is broken, you figure it out' tone. (6) The message should give you hope: convey that the system has self-recovery built in and that the user / agent does not need to manually intervene. The current message produces despair: a person hits this, has no idea what to do, eventually learns to manually 'rm .git/TRACKER_SYNC_PENDING' as cargo-cult ritual, and fears it forever. The right message produces relief — 'oh, this is a known transient state, the system handles it.' Together with properties (1)-(3) from the original description, this defines a fully self-recovering, self-explaining lock-file pattern. The implementation work is small (adopt fcntl.flock + record holder PID + self-recover on stale + improve all four user-facing message strings); the user-experience and trust delta is large."  # f8d5
